DomeGuard 🛡️

DomeGuard is a lightweight, responsive website and domain status monitoring microservice. It continuously audits registered domains, stores diagnostic performance logs (like latency and status codes), and provides system administrators with real-time operational telemetry and outage warnings.

This repository features a Vue.js 3 dashboard frontend, a Spring Boot 3 asynchronous scheduling engine, and a PostgreSQL relational database schema.

🏗️ Architecture Overview

The system operates on a clean 3-tier microservice model:

[ Vue.js 3 Web Client ] <--- HTTP REST ---> [ Spring Boot 3 Engine ] <--- JDBC/JPA ---> [ PostgreSQL Database ]
                                                   |
                                            (Scheduled Thread Pool)
                                                   |
                                                   v
                                         [ Target Web Servers ] (Pings)


Frontend (Vue.js 3 + Tailwind CSS): A high-performance, single-page application monitoring dashboard. Features quick diagnostic controls, real-time logging, and interactive database exploration.

Backend (Spring Boot 3 + Java 17):

Controller Layer: Exposes REST endpoints to manage domains and trigger manual checks.

Scheduled Engine: Employs a fixed-rate Scheduled thread pool executor that wakes up automatically (e.g., every 10 seconds) to audit registered endpoints.

Persistence Layer: Harnesses Spring Data JPA & Hibernate to query/update Postgres targets and insert structured latency records.

Database (PostgreSQL 15+): Stores registered domains and indexes historical latency checks for performance auditing.

✨ Key Features

Real-time Availability Tracking: Auto-resolves HTTP status codes and response latencies using asynchronous multi-threaded checks.

Database Logs Explorer: Full visualization of PostgreSQL relational tables (monitored_domains and latency_logs) directly in the app.

Spring Scheduler Daemon Terminal: Mocked command-line stream displaying Spring engine system events, Hibernate raw inserts, and HTTP clients' successes/timeouts.

On-Demand Diagnoses: Bypass the scheduler to run instantaneous manual force-pings on target endpoints.

Fault Injection Simulation: Simulate downtime or latency spikes to observe database failover response parameters.

🗄️ Database Schema (PostgreSQL)

DomeGuard uses two relational tables linked by foreign keys with active indices on search parameters:

-- 1. Table schema to register websites
CREATE TABLE monitored_domains (
    id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(255) NOT NULL,
    url VARCHAR(512) NOT NULL,
    check_interval INT DEFAULT 10,
    http_method VARCHAR(10) DEFAULT 'GET',
    last_status VARCHAR(15) DEFAULT 'UNKNOWN',
    last_latency BIGINT DEFAULT 0,
    last_checked TIMESTAMP
);

-- 2. Performance metrics historical logs table
CREATE TABLE latency_logs (
    log_id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
    domain_id INT NOT NULL REFERENCES monitored_domains(id) ON DELETE CASCADE,
    checked_at TIMESTAMP DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,
    http_status_code INT,
    latency_ms BIGINT,
    result_state VARCHAR(10)
);

-- Index performance checks for reporting metrics
CREATE INDEX idx_latency_logs_domain ON latency_logs(domain_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_checked_timestamp ON latency_logs(checked_at);


🚀 Getting Started

Follow these steps to set up the backend service and run the application locally.

Prerequisites

Java SDK 17 or higher

Maven 3.8+

PostgreSQL Database running on port 5432

1. Database Setup

Create a new PostgreSQL database and user:

CREATE DATABASE main_analytics;
-- Connect to the database and execute the schema.sql queries listed above.


2. Configure Backend Applications

Locate src/main/resources/application.properties and configure your database credentials:

server.port=8080

# PostgreSQL Connection Properties Setup
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:postgresql://localhost:5432/main_analytics
spring.datasource.username=postgres
spring.datasource.password=YOUR_DATABASE_PASSWORD
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.postgresql.Driver

# JPA & Hibernate Configurations
spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.PostgreSQLDialect
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update
spring.jpa.show-sql=true

# Scheduler Thread pool control properties
spring.task.scheduling.pool.size=5


3. Run Backend (Spring Boot)

From your terminal, compile and run the Spring Boot executable:

mvn clean install
mvn spring-boot:run


The REST API server will begin running on http://localhost:8080.

4. Setup Frontend (Vue.js)

Open the frontend prototype folder. You can serve the Vue files through Nginx or simply run them in an active web browser.
Ensure the base API path in the Vue setup points to your running Spring Boot server:
http://localhost:8080/api/domains

